    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I placed a cage of mussels in a river and collected duplicate eDNA samples at 0.4 km increments downstream of the cage (up to 13 km).  I had 11 positive detection out of of 46 samples. I ran a simple single season occupancy model and then also ran the model with covariates.  The covariates I used were habitat (run, riffle, pool) and distance. Is it appropriate to use distance as a covariate in this situation?  I am aware of the distance modeling-but I don&#39;t think that&#39;s correct here (but this is where I am not sure) <div><br></div><div>My overall goal was to find the probability of detection of mussel eDNA in a 13 km reach, and I want to show how probability of detection increases as one samples closer to the cage. I also had a higher probability of detection in riffle habitats (when analyzed using fishers exact test). </div><div><br></div><div>My final question is-since my p values are relatively high, does that suggest that this was not a good model for my data? Or is it appropriate to rely on the AIC here?</div><div><br></div><div>Below are the results from my run:</div><div>fm1&lt;-occu(~1 ~1,occupancy)<br></div><div><br></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;word-break:break-all\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Occupancy:<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"background-image:initial;background-position:initial;background-size:initial;background-repeat:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;word-break:break-all\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:black;border-width:1pt;border-style:none;border-color:windowtext;padding:0in\">Estimate   SE     z P(&gt;|z|)<u></u><u></u></span><