   "subject": "Re: [unmarked] Distance as a covariate for caged organisms?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Maybe I&#39;m not understanding the design appropriately but this doesn&#39;t seem like a less-than-perfect detection/occupancy problem for which occu addresses.  If the cage of mussels is placed in the river, then it would seem that the whole downstream is by definition &quot;occupied&quot; and it&#39;s only an issue of less-than-perfect detection that almost certainly decreases as one goes downstream.  As such it would seem that a logistic regression would be appropriate.<div><br></div><div>Also, such an analysis would refer to this particular stream (and its structure of pools, riffles, and runs).  With respect to making a general inference to all streams this is a sample of size 1.<br><div><br></div><div>Others should certainly weigh in on this.</div><div><br></div><div>Jim</div></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Tue, Mar 20, 2018 at 4:14 PM, Ellen <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">eppr...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
